# Typesetting Rules

Every paragraph, description, label, button, alt text and data string follows these rules. They are enforced by code, so new copy follows them as long as it goes through `tp()` or is rendered on a page (`TypesetDom`).

## How it is enforced

- **`tp(string)`** in `lib/typeset.ts` applies Tiers 1 to 6 to a string. Wrap data strings in it (`tp("…")`), or wrap copy where it is rendered. Strings that already contain non-breaking spaces pass through safely.
- **`TypesetDom`** (`components/typeset-dom.tsx`) runs `tp()` over every visible text node after render, and again on each DOM change.
  - It only swaps normal spaces for non-breaking ones and never changes the words.
  - It skips `title`, `meta`, `script`, `style`, `noscript`, `textarea`, `input`, `select`, `option`, `code`, `pre`, `svg` and contenteditable elements.
  - Opt an element out with `data-no-typeset`.
- **CSS** (`globals.css`):
  - `text-wrap: balance` and `overflow-wrap: break-word` on h1–h6;
  - `text-wrap: pretty` on body text;
  - `hyphens: manual`.

  These help the rag, but they don't replace the binding.

## Tier 1: one-letter words

Bind `a` and `I` to the **following** word.

```tsx
// BAD
{"drove a number."}
// GOOD
{"drove a number."}
```

## Tier 2: short function words

These never sit alone at the end of a line. Bind each to the **following** word. The list in code (`SHORT` in `lib/typeset.ts`):

`a i of at in on to or by an as is it if so up we no do be he my the and for but nor yet our its his her your their with from into per via`

```tsx
// BAD: "of" or "in" can dangle at line end
the head of creative at a global agency in Dubai
// GOOD
the head of creative at a global agency in Dubai
```

## Tier 3: sentence-start protection

After `.` `?` `!` (and a closing quote, if there is one), if the **next word is 4 letters or fewer**, bind it to the word after it, so a new sentence never starts with 1–2 stranded words. This covers `We`, `It`, `He`, `So`, `My`, `But`, `And`, `The`, `Yet`, `For`, `Now`, `How`, `Who`, `One`, `All`, `Our`, `You`, `Two`, `New`, `Then`, `That`, `This`, `What`, `When` and every other word of 4 letters or fewer.

```tsx
// BAD
{"Several times, actually. We tested."}
// GOOD
{"Several times, actually. We tested."}
```

## Tier 4: clause connectors

After `,` or `;`, if the next word is a conjunction (`and`, `but`, `or`, `nor`, `yet`, `so`), bind it to the word after it.

## Tier 5: widow prevention

The last line of every paragraph contains **at least two words**: bind the final two words. Lines of one or two words are left alone. `tp()` treats each `\n` as a paragraph break.

## Tier 6: number binding

Bind a number to its unit or noun: `13 years`, `60 days`, `4,000 kilometres`, `$8,000 month`, `12% increase`, `50+ people`.

In code, a number is digits with optional `,` `.` and a trailing `%`, `+` or `k`. A number-word compound keeps its hyphen unbreakable: `25-person` becomes `25‑person`.

## The overflow guard

A chain of bound words can't wrap, so a long chain would overflow a narrow column or a large heading. `tp()` splits any unbreakable run **longer than 18 characters** (`MAX_RUN`) from the right. It keeps only the strongest bonds: words in the `SHORT` list and numbers stay with the word after them, and the last two words stay together.

## Non-breaking space syntax

| Context | Syntax | Example |
|---------|--------|---------|
| Inside a JS string `{"..."}` | ` ` | `{"drove a number."}` |
| Plain JSX text | `&nbsp;` | `See the&nbsp;work` |
| Data objects (`.ts` arrays) | ` `, or wrap in `tp()` | `desc: tp("Built to last.")` |

## Punctuation

- **Ranges:** en dash with no spaces, `2–3`, `2019–2023`. Use a spaced en dash only between two spaced items: `Dec 2024 – Jun 2025`.
- **Separators:** middot with spaces, `2024 · Creative Director`.
- **Quotes and apostrophes:** always curly (`“ ” ‘ ’`, `’` in strings).
- **Ellipsis:** `…`, one character.
- **Uppercase:** only through CSS (`uppercase`) with `--tracking-label` or `--tracking-sublabel`. Never type copy in capitals.
- Case, numbers, dates, money and units: see "Copy style" in `design-system.mdc`.

## Text measure

Use `max-w-[60ch]` on paragraph containers (about 55–75 characters a line). Use `max-w-[65ch]` only for article body reading.

## Checklist for new text

1. Scan for standalone `a` and `I`, and bind them (Tier 1).
2. Scan for the short words in the `SHORT` list, and bind them (Tier 2).
3. Check sentence boundaries for starters of 4 letters or fewer (Tier 3).
4. Check each comma or semicolon followed by a conjunction (Tier 4).
5. Check the last line of every paragraph has 2+ words (Tier 5).
6. Bind numbers to their units (Tier 6).
7. Check headings at 375px: no bound chain should overflow (the guard splits runs over 18 characters).
8. Check case, numbers and spelling against "Copy style" (sentence case, figures for proof, UK English).
